Z||000000||fiero_hunter85||09-14-2001||06:15 PM||||i am getting ready to remove the 2.8 from my 85 GT 4 speed and i looked at my engine compartment and it looks like i might have the room to pull the engine out from the top if i remove all of the pully's.<BR> will it work? has anyone ever done this?<BR> <BR>another thing i need to know is if there is any other GM car that uses the same air filter canister as the fiero? mine is rusted out. <P>my last question is if there is any other car that has the same alternator bracket as the fiero?<P><BR>if the answers to all my questions are no that i am really in trouble since there are no V6 fiero's at any bone yards here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||My7Fieros||09-14-2001||06:37 PM||||1. Engine out the top question...there have been a few people on the forum that have discussed it or even actually done it...however, its a general consensus here that it is MUCH easier to take it out the bottom.....really.<P>2. Air canister...not to my knowledge. Go to a boneyard. I <I>believe</I> that all Fieros, even the 4 bangers, use the same air filter canister. (Someone correct me if Im wrong, I dont own any 4 banger Fieros.)<P>3. Alt brackets...again, to my knowledge, no. Fiero engine components were pretty much designed specifically for the Fiero and its mid engine trans mounted design.<P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B><I>My 7 Fieros</FONT></FONT></B></I>||||reg||1
Z||000002||frankshel||09-14-2001||06:51 PM||||I took a V-6 out through the top. It does work but you have to "bare bones " the block...remove everything in the way.<BR>Not as bad as everyone thinks.<BR>The benefit of taking it out through the bottom is that you can put it back in pretty much intact. You also won't need a chainfall by going out the bottom.<P>I have 5 Fiero's and I do not like to replace engines!<P>Frank Sheldon<BR>Ticonderoga, Ny||||reg||1
Z||000003||Banzai||09-15-2001||10:26 AM||||You will be much happier taking it out of the bottom. I used an engine hoist to lower it out of the body.<P>The air filter housings are different on the four and the six. try a housing out of a v6 Corsica, Cavilier, or late 80's to mid 90's Buick Regal. most 2.8's and 3.1's use the canister type air filter housing. I'm not exactly sure if the mount are different but just some info to take to the boneyard.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero_hunter85||09-15-2001||11:01 AM||||ok, thanks for replying.  i only have a 1 car garage so it would be pretty hard to get the engine and cradle out from under the body.  the reason i need a new filter canister is because after sitting for 5 years it got about 10 rust holes in it. i drove it home with a filter that falls apart when you touch it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||09-15-2001||01:33 PM||||I have never pulled the engine from the top, but I can have it out in about 3 hours from the bottem. It looks like it is soooo much easier then try to pull it out the top. I jack the car up slightly, slide a pallet with 4 large wood blocks to meet the cradle, under it, lower the car onto it, unbolt the cradle & suspension, then lift the car with an engine hoist over the engine, roll the engine out from under it, lower it until I'm ready to reinstall.||||reg||1
